Mod[ified] duration is a measure of the average life of a bond, weighting each repayment by the time until it will be made and[ reflecting] the fact that money flows in the near future are more valuable than the same money flows at a later date. Modified duration indicates how changes in interest rates will affect the price of a bond (or bond portfolio). The longer the duration of a bond, the greater the extent to which its price is affected by interest rate changes. As such modified duration is used as a measure of risk for bond portfolios.
